{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.OcneanuTrace","lean:module:QOU.Mass.HeckeStokesEigenvalues","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":13,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_coxeter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_coxeter","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L132-L140","doc":"**Coxeter presentation.** The three relations of `S_n` on the shadow generators, bundled:\ninvolution, braid, far commutation. This is the content of \"the classical shadow is a\npermutation representation\", which the probe measured as `S₃` at `n = 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_isUnit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_isUnit","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L127-L130","doc":"Each shadow generator is a unit, its own inverse (a transposition). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_far_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_far_comm","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L120-L125","doc":"The shadow satisfies far commutation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_braid","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L113-L118","doc":"The shadow satisfies the braid relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_kills_h","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_kills_h","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L94-L104","doc":"**The shadow forgets exactly the deformation.** Pushing the Hecke quadratic through `ε` and\nusing the involution gives `ε((q − q⁻¹) • g) = 0`: the classical target sees `h = 0`, i.e. the\n`q = 1` specialisation. What Step 2 leaves stipulated is therefore the deformation `1 ↦ q` alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_hecke_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_hecke_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L88-L92","doc":"The classical shadow satisfies the Hecke quadratic at `q = 1`: `(X − 1)(X + 1) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.hecke_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.hecke_factor","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L71-L86","doc":"**The two Stokes/Hecke eigenvalues are a consequence.** Every generator factors as\n`(g − q)(g + q⁻¹) = 0` in `A` — the algebra behind `stokes_curvature_factors`, now derived from\nthe monodromy hypothesis instead of stipulated as a curvature. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_involution","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.shadow_involution","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L64-L64","doc":"The classical shadow of every generator is an involution: the branch-point transposition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.far_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.far_comm","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L62-L62","doc":"Far commutation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.braid","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L60-L60","doc":"The braid relation between neighbours. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.quad","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.quad","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L58-L58","doc":"The Hecke quadratic on every generator, with `h = q − q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy.mk","kind":"ctor","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L55-L55","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.HeckeMonodromy","kind":"class","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/HeckeMonodromy.lean#L49-L64","doc":"**Conjecture (Step 2 as monodromy).** For a Markov-trace tower `T : MarkovTrace K A` with\ngenerators `T.g i`, a parameter `q ≠ 0`, and a ring map `ε : A →+* B` to the classical target:\nthe generators are the monodromies of the substrate connection around the branch points of the\ncolour cubic, so they satisfy the Hecke quadratic with `h = q − q⁻¹`, the braid and far-commutation\nrelations of the braid group, and under `ε` each becomes an involution (the Picard–Lefschetz\ntransposition on the two colliding roots). ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:conj:hecke-monodromy-step-two","label":"conj:hecke-monodromy-step-two","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/hecke-monodromy-step-two.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}